@meta
id: 29-15
title: Git output is just text in a buffer
chapter: 29
type: lesson
skills: fugitive
par: 120
minutes: 8
vscode: the Source Control panel, which you cannot pipe anywhere
open: CHANGELOG.md

@brief
No plugin is shipped with this lesson, which is the lesson.

Fugitive is the git plugin everyone recommends, and the thing worth
understanding about it is what it is not. It is not a git client with a
panel and buttons. It runs git, puts the output in a vim buffer, and adds
mappings on top of that buffer. `:Git status` gives you a buffer of
statuses where `-` stages a file. `:Gdiffsplit` gives you two buffers and
vim's own diff mode. `:Git blame` gives you a buffer of blame lines
beside your code. The value is that git's output becomes text you can
search, yank, filter and edit with every key you already know.

You can have the underlying half of that today with no plugin at all.
`:r !cmd` reads a command's output into the buffer. Chapter 26 taught it;
this lesson points it at git.

The release notes for 0.4.0 are empty. `config.v3.ts` and `config.v4.ts`
are the payments config before and after. Put the three changed settings
under `### Changed`, one per line, as bullets:

    - retryLimit: 7
    - backoffMs: 1000
    - deadLetterTopic: 'payment.dead'

Get them out of git rather than reading the two files and typing. Both
config files must be left exactly as they are.

@keys
| `:r !{cmd}` | read a command's output in below the cursor |
| `:!{cmd}` | run it and just look at the output |
| `git diff --no-index a b` | diff two files with no repository involved |
| `-U0` | no context lines, only the changes |
| `` `[ `` and `` `] `` | the first and last line `:r` just inserted |

@hints
`git diff` works on two loose files if you pass `--no-index`. Read its
output straight into the buffer instead of looking at it.
--
The diff has a header and `-` lines you do not want. You can filter it in
the shell before vim ever sees it, with a pipe, or in vim afterwards.
--
`G`, then
`:r !git diff --no-index -U0 config.v3.ts config.v4.ts | grep "^+[^+]"`,
then a substitute over the lines you just read to turn `+  retryLimit: 7,`
into `- retryLimit: 7`. `:'[,']` is the range for exactly those lines.

@solution
    G
    :r !git diff --no-index -U0 config.v3.ts config.v4.ts | grep "^+[^+]"<Enter>
    :'[,']s/^+\s*\(.*\),/- \1/<Enter>
    :wq<Enter>

About a hundred keystrokes, most of them the command, and none of them a
value you could get wrong. Read the values out of the file by eye and you
will type `1000` as `100` about one time in twenty, and the changelog is
the one place nobody rereads.

Three ideas are stacked here and all three are worth keeping separately.

**Git's output is text.** `git diff`, `git log --oneline`, `git blame`,
`git ls-files` — all of them print lines. Once lines are in a buffer they
are yours: `:g/^-/d`, `:sort`, a macro, a substitution. That is why
fugitive feels different from a source-control panel, and it is available
to you before you install fugitive.

**The shell pipeline is part of the tool.** `| grep "^+[^+]"` threw away
the header and the deletions before vim saw them. You could have done it
in vim with `:g`, and on a big diff the pipe is faster and shorter.
Chapter 26 gave you `!`, `:r !` and `:w !`; git is just another command
on the other end of them.

**`'[` and `']` mark what you just inserted.** Every command that brings
text into a buffer sets them, so "operate on exactly what I just pasted"
is always available as a range. It is the least-known useful thing in
this lesson.

When you do install fugitive, install it for the parts you cannot rebuild
in a line: `:Git blame` in a split that scrolls with your file, staging
individual hunks from a diff buffer, and `:Gread` to drop a file back to
its committed state. Not for `:Git status`, which is `:!git status`.

@start CHANGELOG.md
# Changelog

## 0.4.0 — payments

### Changed

@start config.v3.ts
export const paymentsConfig = {
  retryLimit: 3,
  backoffMs: 250,
  deadLetterTopic: 'payment.failed',
};

@start config.v4.ts
export const paymentsConfig = {
  retryLimit: 7,
  backoffMs: 1000,
  deadLetterTopic: 'payment.dead',
};

@expected CHANGELOG.md
